<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Hilal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Banu Hilal were an Arab tribal confederation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> who migrated through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the 11th century, encouraged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> to punish the Zirids for breaking with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. From the 1050s they defeated the Zirids, ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> and pushed the Hammadids to the coast. Their arrival spread Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and inspired the epic Taghribat Bani Hilal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banu-hilal.jpg" alt="Banu Hilal" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SPQR10 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pre_Islamic_Arabian_Tribes_(Harold_Dixon).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Banu Hilal were an Arab tribe from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> region of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, sometimes travelling towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in search of pastures and oases. According to Arab genealogists they were a sub-tribe of the Mudar confederation, specifically of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> ibn Sa'sa'a, and their progenitor was Hilal. They were very numerous, effectively a nation divided into sub-tribes, of which the most notable were the Athbaj, Riyah, Jusham, Zughba, Adi and Qurra.</p><p>They probably did not accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> until after Muhammad's victory at the Battle of Hunayn in 630, and like other tribes of their group they did not join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ridda-wars">Ridda Wars</a>. In the early 8th century some of them moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and in the 9th century they migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 970s the Hilal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-sulaym">Banu Sulaym</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> in their attacks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>. After his victory in 978 the Fatimid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> forcibly relocated the two tribes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, where they were forbidden to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. They plundered the region as far as the port of Aydhab. According to al-Tijani, they refused to cross the Nile until each warrior received a dinar and a fur garment, after which around 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> departed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>.</p><p>The migration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> began after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty">Zirid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> declared independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. As a punishment, the Fatimids sent the Banu Hilal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-sulaym">Banu Sulaym</a> west, giving each man a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> and money, and a severe drought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> added to the pressure to leave. Estimates of the number of nomads who moved in the 11th century run from 50,000 to 250,000, and some scholars propose much higher figures. They defeated the Zirids after the battle of Haydaran, and the Zirids abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> for the coast. In the second half of the 12th century the Hilal reached the Moulouya valley and the Atlantic coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Their arrival was a major factor in the linguistic, cultural and ethnic Arabization of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and spread nomadism in areas where agriculture had prevailed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> wrote that lands ravaged by the invaders had become desert. Originally Isma'ili Shia, most of the tribe adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> in the Maghreb. In the 17th century the Moroccan sultan Ismail Ibn Sharif built a guich army from Hilal and Banu Maqil warriors.</p><p>Their journey is told in the Taghribat Bani Hilal, an Arab epic gathered by the folk poet Abdul Rahman al-Abnudi from the bards of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>. It was performed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> until the early 20th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banu Hilal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hafsid Ifriqiya combined an urban, literate society on the coast with Arab and Berber tribal groups in the steppe and the interior, many descended from the <b>Banu Hilal</b> and Banu Sulaym who had entered the region in the eleventh century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…uniformly Islamised cities, where politics ran along the lines of local aristocratic families, and a countryside that stayed largely Berber and tribal; the nomads, Berber tribes joined by Arab ones such as the <b>Banu Hilal</b> who had arrived under the Almohads, became more Arabised than the settled peasantry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Banu Hilal</b> invasions of the 1050s shattered the Zirid emirate, local lords took control of the cities of Ifriqiya.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-jami-emirate-of-gabes">Banu Jami emirate of Gabes</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Banu Hilal</b> defeated the Zirid emirate and Kairouan was abandoned in 1057, Tunis broke away.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-khurasan">Banu Khurasan</a></li><li><blockquote>Pressure from the <b>Banu Hilal</b> led al-Nasir ibn Alnas to build a new capital at Béjaïa (Bougie) on the coast in the 1060s, which became a busy port trading with Italian cities.</blockquote><a href="/state/hammadid-emirate">Hammadid emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>In revenge the Fatimids encouraged the <b>Banu Hilal</b> and Banu Sulaym Arab tribes to move west.</blockquote><a href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
